From 1727d68bff0a4d49999f32c7ec45a49069b869b5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Fexiven <48439988+Fexiven@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 09:55:08 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 10/11] accel/rocket: scale the NPU clock and supply with
 devfreq

The RK3576 NPU has an OPP table in the vendor device tree, from 300 MHz
at 725 mV to 950 MHz at 875 mV, with the clock from the firmware's
PVTPLL (SCMI). Without it the cores run from the CRU clock at one fixed
rate.

When a core has "operating-points-v2", the first such core gets the
table, with the optional "pvtpll" clock as the OPP clock and "npu-supply"
as the OPP supply, and a devfreq device with the simple_ondemand
governor. The cores share the clock and the supply, so the device stands
for both: it counts the NPU as busy while any core runs a job, and
powering up and down follows the first and the last core.

The PVTPLL does not survive the NPU power domain powering off: the bus
hangs when the domain comes back with it selected. So before the last
core powers down, devfreq is suspended and the clock is parked at
200 MHz, a rate the firmware takes from the CRU, then the OPP supply is
released. When the first core powers up, the OPP devfreq chose last is
set again (the OPP core raises the voltage before the clock) and devfreq
resumes. While the cores are off, a new rate is only recorded.

The NPU is busy for 15-55 % of an inference for most models, as the CPU
runs part of the model and prepares the jobs, and the cores power down
50 ms after the last job. With the governor's defaults (upthreshold 45,
50 ms) detection models stayed at 300-600 MHz; upthreshold 20,
downdifferential 10 and a 100 ms interval give the latency of a fixed
950 MHz.

Without an OPP table, or without a usable supply, the NPU runs from its
CRU clock as before.

The device tree properties are described by the previous patch,
"dt-bindings: npu: rockchip,rknn: add RK3576 PVTPLL clock and OPP
table": the optional seventh clock "pvtpll" and "operating-points-v2"
for rockchip,rk3576-rknn-core; "npu-supply" exists already.

Tested on RK3576S: under load the NPU runs at up to 950 MHz / 875 mV,
when idle the clock is parked at 200 MHz with the supply off, and all
test models give bit-identical results at every OPP.

Co-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
